{{Short description|Administrative divisions of Guajicutea}}

The '''districts of the Republic of Guajicutea''' are the island state's sixteen mainland districtos plus the separate island district of [[Warikú]]. The modern system descends from the colonial territorial divisions created from the feudal [[bikabá]] system and was retained after the 1947 constitution because it remained useful for administration, census reporting, and legislative apportionment. In everyday politics, district identity still matters: municipal government, local patronage, and Assembly elections all run through district loyalties. On current figures, the districts together elect 284 members to the [[Asamblea Nacional of Guajicutea|Asamblea Nacional]], apportioned roughly one seat per 10,000 residents.

Most Guajicuteans live in a dense coastal and urban belt running from [[Waribá]] through [[Tabakurí]] to [[Barikú]], while the inland and highland districts are much thinner. The Waribá urban area spills across district boundaries, so the largest built-up concentration is larger than any single district on its own.

